WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.140
DINO CHIESA: Welcome,
and thanks for joining.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.050
I'm glad you're all here.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.700
My name is Dino Chiesa.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.050
I work for Google
on the Apigee team.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.610
My role is to help customers
understand and use Apigee

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.640
to solve their
business problems.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.400
Today, we're going to talk
about service mesh and API

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.880
management, and how T-Mobile
adopted both of these

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.610
to improve their capability
to deliver innovation

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.350
securely and at scale.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.890
This isn't news,
enterprises are evolving

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:37.900
their monolithic
applications into

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.180
microservices architectures.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.570
Modeling a system as a set of
interconnected microservices

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.110
helps organizations to build and
maintain complex, distributed

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:49.130
systems more easily.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:51.460
The theory is small
grained services can

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.040
be independently developed,
versioned, deployed,

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.810
scaled, and updated, meaning a
more flexible, agile approach

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.940
as the system grows.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.569
It's an attractive idea, and
service mesh infrastructure

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.820
has been built to
help manage these sets

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.760
of inter-cooperating
services, lightweight proxies,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.850
like Linkerd or Envoy,
management tools built

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:14.620
on top of these.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:16.750
These allow the services
to find each other,

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:20.380
communicate securely,
and scale elastically.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.990
But no enterprise,
no matter how modern,

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.560
consists only of systems
built on microservices.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.230
That's one category
in a portfolio,

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:36.160
which includes custom monoliths
and third-party SaaS systems.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.110
Now, a service is only
useful if developers

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:42.550
can find it, get the credentials
required to connect to it,

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.800
and then build and run
an application using

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.900
those credentials.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.050
For good hygiene, we've
got to manage and measure

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:51.980
all of that activity.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.360
This is where API
management comes in.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.010
It helps you manage
the APIs that are

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:59.440
shared outside of each domain.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.110
And remember, sharing
could be across domains

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:03.010
within a company.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.310
Now, I want to talk a little
bit about API management

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.030
and its enabling of
modernization, that transition

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.270
from monoliths to microservices.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.830
One capability of an
API management system

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:21.030
is to intelligently route
requests from clients

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.370
to backend systems.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.560
When you introduce
a new system, you

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.550
can use the API management
layer to migrate those requests.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.550
This is a really common use
case among our customers.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:38.140
The routing can be dependent on
any factor, and can be dynamic.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.990
Client applications can
be completely unaware

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:44.963
that this is happening, and that
facilitates a phased migration

00:02:44.963 --> 00:02:45.505
with control.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.590
Apigee is API management,
and it's cross-cloud,

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.880
allowing you to manage APIs
across your entire portfolio--

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.660
your own data center, public
clouds, third-party SaaS,

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.180
wherever the service is run.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.160
With that, now I'd like
to invite my friend

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:06.700
Terry to talk about T-Mobile's
modernization journey,

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.190
and how they used API
management and service

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:13.180
mesh as complementary
pieces of their foundation.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.990
Terry?

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:16.280
TERRY WANG: Thank you, Dino.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.200
Hi, my name is Terry Wang.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.980
I work on API management and
service mesh in API center

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.140
for the enablement
team at T-Mobile.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.650
I'm glad to be here.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:30.160
First, I want to talk about
our API first journey.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.270
To be honest, it's been quite
a journey for us at T-Mobile.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:36.620
We introduced Apigee
six years ago,

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.640
to jump-start our API program.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.390
Back then, we had very
few microservices, or even

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.070
RESTful services.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:48.330
Majority of the
services were SOA based,

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.370
delivered by middleware
platforms or custom Java

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:52.950
applications.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:57.760
We use Apigee gateway mainly
to transform the SOA payloads

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.390
into JSON to make them
easier for the UI application

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:02.830
to consume.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.350
Four years ago,
T-Mobile embarked

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.300
on the digital
transformation journey

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.980
and made the API first strategy
as one of its main drivers.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:17.290
We started to break down
monolithic into microservices.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:21.279
We had tremendous growth, not
only in number of services,

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:24.010
but also in business agility.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.040
It used to take months
to make changes.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.815
Now, many apps are
releasing daily.

00:04:29.815 --> 00:04:31.990
It's so rosy, right?

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.650
Yes, but we definitely
experienced the growing pain.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:40.420
We still had multiple platforms,
languages, and libraries.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:43.300
Security was still
difficult to implement,

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:47.790
and observability and
delivery were inconsistent.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:51.660
That's when we started to
look into Istio service mesh.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:55.290
We successfully deployed our
pilot services into production

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.830
last year.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.580
Now, we're planning to
roll out in big waves,

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.210
in both our public
and private clouds.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:07.010
Since our team
started two years ago,

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.970
we've been working on
building developer tools

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:15.040
to bootstrap API development in
every phase of the lifecycle.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.130
We have [INAUDIBLE]
in design phase

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:21.700
to make sure the API design
standards are followed.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.310
In development phase,
we provide teams

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:27.990
with Apigee proxy and
Istio mesh generators.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.700
In build and
deployment phase, we

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.700
give domain teams
the CI/CD pipelines

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.650
with compliance checks.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:40.120
These tools allow development
team to deploy their services

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.070
into Apigee and service
mesh in a secure

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.150
and [INAUDIBLE] manner.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:50.160
In every step in the API
development lifecycle,

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.540
the events are logged.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.770
Metadata is collected.

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:57.460
And they are searchable through
our API Explorer tool, where

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.730
both API consumers
and providers can

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:05.560
find the relative information
about their services quickly.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:10.610
This improves our [? meantime ?]
to recovery when issues occur.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:12.760
We're very proud
of these tools that

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:18.090
enable us at T-Mobile running
our API program at scale.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:22.480
What really motivated us to
look into Istio service mesh?

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:26.920
Well, the shift left insecurity
strategy a couple of years ago.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:31.110
We wanted to enforce strong
token authentication for all

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:35.550
the APIs with proper logging
and monitoring for any security

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.200
violations.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.340
We created token
libraries for Java

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:43.020
and made it available to
the development teams.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:45.060
It worked well in some degree.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:49.530
We had hundreds of
services adopt the library.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:53.260
However, there's still
thousands of APIs left

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:56.130
and many programming
languages to cover.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:59.610
That got us into
Istio, which provides

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:03.510
security and granular metrics
we're looking for, and more,

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:04.960
out of the box.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:10.290
The features such as circuit
breakers and security handling,

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.050
connection timeout and
retrying, and telemetries

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:16.900
are often handled by
development team in the code.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:20.330
Depends on programming languages
and libraries, and project

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:22.150
timelines, sometimes.

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:25.500
These features were
implemented in different ways

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.910
and managed by different
tools that were inconsistent,

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:33.850
a big overhead for teams
to manage and operate.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:36.170
With a service
mesh, these features

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:41.380
are offered at platform level.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:45.190
Using standard policies, the
overhead for these events

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:49.310
features shifts from
developers to the service mesh.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.750
It helps development teams
deliver product features

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:57.210
and deliver them faster
and more securely.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:01.200
Service mesh and API management
solve two different problems.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.250
Together, it transforms
your backend services

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:09.330
into APIs that are consumable
by internal and external

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.070
applications.

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.830
The service mesh
gives more details

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:18.420
to service clients about the
topology of the architecture,

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:22.010
the resilience mechanisms
they should implement,

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:25.330
and granular telemetry
they should collect,

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:28.440
as security flows
that participate.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:33.510
All these implementation details
are provided to application

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.409
by Envoy cycle process.

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:38.630
With API management,
we're trying

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:41.600
to solve the problem
of when we wish

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.220
to expose existing APIs for
others to consume, how do we

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:47.320
track?

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.760
Who uses this API?

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.550
And enforce policies
about who's allowed

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:56.720
to use them, and establish
security flows to authenticate

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:01.430
authorized permissible use,
and build a catalog of services

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:06.820
that can be used at the design
time to promote API usage.

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.200
At T-Mobile, our
services are built

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.300
in different sizes and forms.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.390
They are deployed in
various platforms,

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:17.300
in on-prem cloud
or public cloud.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:22.820
API management layer allows us
to provide consistent influence

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:25.610
to the consumers and
lays the foundation

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:27.950
for effective governance.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:31.220
API gateway and service
mesh architecture

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:36.650
is the paved road at
T-Mobile for API development.

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:41.630
Next, I'm going to demo
our Apigee proxy generator

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:44.150
and use your mesh
generator tools

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:46.970
that enable developers
to deliver services

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:52.620
faster with a strong security
by default. In this session,

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:55.010
I'm going to demo a
couple of generator tools

00:09:55.010 --> 00:10:00.800
in our tool suite, Istio
config gen and Apigee ProxyGen.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.580
The purpose of these generators
is to let developers quickly

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.180
get onto the platform and
take advantage of the platform

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:12.080
features right away without
spending too much time to learn

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:16.910
it, so that they can
focus more on developing

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:19.100
their domain-specific features.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:23.600
This generator bundle, a
default set of features

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.200
often required to make
this service more secure

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:32.060
and follow the best practices
set forth by the company.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:33.860
Definitely,
developers can always

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:37.160
add to their custom
policies and configurations

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:40.980
that meet their specific
requirements later.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:44.130
Out of the box, the
default should already

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:48.270
provide 70% to 80%
of the features.

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:52.300
OK, let's get into it.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:56.900
Let's look at the
Istio mesh gen first.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:00.630
We always follow the
API first approach.

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:04.660
So the mesh config generator
is actually a microservice,

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:07.070
a RESTful service.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:13.850
It will generate the Spring
Boot app, the Istio home charts,

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:16.890
and a default CI/CD pipeline.

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:21.320
And then we use a home chart
to deploy the configurations

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:23.030
