the features, and
you can quickly

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:27.160
deploy your microservice
into the cluster.

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:35.040
Secondly, I want to demo
the Apigee ProxyGen.

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:38.110
Just from a swagger
file, and you

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:44.110
can specify the Apigee proxy
names and some other vaulting

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:50.650
rules, and will generate
a default set of policies.

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:55.150
And then you can deploy it
into any Apigee environment.

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:59.750
Whether or not it's SaaS, or
on-prem, it doesn't matter.

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:03.160
So with this
approach, we achieved

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:08.440
end-to-end connectivity
and security.

00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:14.780
So let me show the
generator a little bit.

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:22.359
Just to save time, I already
built this configuration

00:17:22.359 --> 00:17:23.410
already.

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:27.310
And everything is
based on this swagger.

00:17:27.310 --> 00:17:31.680
And given the swagger will have
the default values for you,

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:37.840
you specify where do you want
to store the generated project,

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:43.520
and how to route to targets,
and what the project information

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:45.970
does for tracking as well.

00:17:45.970 --> 00:18:02.020
And finally, so you can submit,
will generate a Apigee CI/CD

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:03.650
pipeline for you.

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:06.920
So over here, it generated
the proxy endpoint.

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.280
It generated the
target endpoints.

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:14.710
And then pipeline generated.

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:19.690
And then we'll do quite a
few compliance of checks,

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:23.200
and the authentication
authorization checks,

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:25.360
and threat protection checks.

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:31.060
And loggings, we definitely
need, and also the quota

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:36.700
and the SpikeArrest
for traffic policies.

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:42.200
And then you can just deploy
to any environment you want.

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:46.255
So you can customize this
to add more as you wish.

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:57.380
So I want to show you
what the policies looks

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:02.300
like when it deploy into
the Apigee environment.

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:05.210
So you can see that
by default, we'll

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:12.440
use the shared flow for
many of these policies.

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:17.600
And there's a default set
of policies for PreFlow

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:22.670
and another for set of policies
for Post Client, PostFlow.

00:19:22.670 --> 00:19:25.890
And then there is
logging policies

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:28.010
in the Post Client flow.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:33.280
So without you knowing
anything about Apigee,

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:39.830
you are able to just expose
your service on the Apigee

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:42.365
and expose to other
teams to consume.

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:50.995
Now, let's look at the
end-to-end testing.

00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:58.550
So I do have this
service up here.

00:19:58.550 --> 00:20:02.780
This is configured,
exposed through the Apigee,

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:08.260
and connect to the service
I just deployed in the Istio

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:09.760
environment.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:17.200
So without the token,
I should get 401.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:20.880
So this is from Apigee,
right, so not allowing

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:23.880
to pass to the backend at all.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:26.250
There's a security protection.

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:34.240
If I do have the
[? joe ?] token,

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:36.880
I'm passing through
Apigee layer and getting

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:39.770
into the Istio service mesh.

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:42.980
And I'm getting the
501 not implemented,

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:45.200
which is the correct response.

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:51.400
So if I give the
wrong token, I'm

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:55.970
getting a more generic message.

00:20:55.970 --> 00:21:01.360
So this is when you
expose through Apigee

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:04.810
and you're getting
more unified error

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:12.420
messages for all these services
exposed through Apigee.

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:23.410
So the third feature I want to
demo is our API Explorer tool.

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:26.530
So we collect all
the informations,

00:21:26.530 --> 00:21:30.570
every step of the process,
including the generator,

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:33.010
including the deployment.

00:21:33.010 --> 00:21:41.530
From the API Explorer, you
can get all those informations

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:44.770
from swagger specification,
to where it deployed,

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:47.860
and who's consuming,
and who owns this,

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:51.830
and what's the revision history.

00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:55.360
So one of the things
I do want to show

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:58.420
is the telemetry, right.

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:06.370
So this is the Apigee proxy
I deployed today by me.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:09.715
And I want to see the metrics.

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:19.200
Since I deployed yesterday,
I did some testings today.

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:26.510
So it shows all the
details about this service,

00:22:26.510 --> 00:22:30.710
and shows how it performs,
and what error I can get.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:38.930
This is the end of my
demo, which shows you

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:44.780
how to use the generators to
deploy your service into Istio

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:47.090
and expose through Apigee.

00:22:47.090 --> 00:22:51.890
And you're getting a default
set of features and also

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:54.920
the telemetries you're getting.

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:57.350
Thank you very much.

00:22:57.350 --> 00:22:58.850
DINO CHIESA: Thanks
for that, Terry.

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:00.890
It's super interesting
to learn about T-Mobile's

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:03.050
journey towards
greater flexibility

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:05.480
and agility in
their systems, which

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:09.860
brings me to the last thing I'd
like to mention, Apigee hybrid.

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:12.710
When customers use the
Apigee SaaS gateway, which

00:23:12.710 --> 00:23:15.170
runs in the Google Cloud, the
connection from the client,

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:17.090
to the gateway, to
the upstream service

00:23:17.090 --> 00:23:20.540
can span multiple
public networks.

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:24.140
With Apigee hybrid, you can run
at Apigee gateway as co-located

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:25.410
with your workloads.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:27.560
This flexibility
means you can avoid

00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:30.310
crossing network boundaries,
while still enjoying

00:23:30.310 --> 00:23:32.450
seeing great Apigee capability.

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:36.800
So consider that if you
need that capability.

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:38.810
Finally, I'd like to summarize.

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:40.760
A couple of things
we learned today,

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:44.930
API management complements
microservices architectures.

00:23:44.930 --> 00:23:46.760
Second, T-Mobile's
journey combines

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:50.060
API management and service
mesh with processes and tooling

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:53.340
to produce a platform on which
T-Mobile developers build

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:55.230
the future.

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:58.543
And finally, Apigee hybrid
provides even more flexibility

00:23:58.543 --> 00:23:59.460
for those who need it.

